INSERT INTO blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, keywords, cover_image, published, created_at, updated_at, word_count, content_chars)
VALUES ('ai-chatbot-car-rental-services', 'AI Chatbot for Car Rentals: Stop Losing Bookings at Night', 'Answer instantly, quote rates, follow up on quotes and confirm pickups - the full playbook for small car rental companies.', '[{"type":"img","src":"/blog/car-rental-services.jpg","alt":"Traveler calling a car rental company late at night at the airport with luggage while the rental desk phone rings unanswered","caption":"A stranded traveler at the airport can''t reach the car rental company at night."},{"type":"p","text":"It is 11:48 PM on a Thursday. A flight from London has just landed, forty minutes late. Sarah walks into the arrivals hall with two suitcases and a sleeping four-year-old on her shoulder. Her rental car was supposed to be waiting. The confirmation email said \"call us when you land.\" So she calls. It rings. And rings. Voicemail. She calls again, in case she pressed the wrong number. Voicemail again. The hotel shuttle left twenty minutes ago, and the taxis outside want a fortune for the long ride."},{"type":"p","text":"She opens her phone, types \"car rental near me open now,\" and calls the second number that comes up. A tired man answers. He has a sedan available, and he sends a driver to the arrivals curb in fifteen minutes. By the time her original company calls back at 9 the next morning, she has already paid someone else, and she has already told her sister never to book with them. She will not call them again. Not for this trip. Not for the next one either."},{"type":"p","text":"This scene plays out every single night at airports and train stations everywhere. The rental company did not lose her because of bad cars or bad prices. They lost her because nobody picked up the phone. An AI chatbot fixes this exact moment. It answers every call and every message, instantly, at any hour. It takes the booking, quotes the rate, confirms the car, and sends the pickup details, all while the owner is asleep. Let us walk through how this works for a small, independent car rental company."},{"type":"h2","text":"The after-hours booking leak"},{"type":"p","text":"Here is a hard truth about the car rental business. The people who call you after hours are your best customers. Nobody calls a car rental company at midnight for fun. A midnight caller has a flight landing, a train arriving, or an emergency that needs a car first thing in the morning. They have already done their research. They have already decided. They are calling to hand you money, and they want to do it right now."},{"type":"p","text":"And yet, after-hours calls are exactly the calls small rental companies miss most. The big names run twenty-four-hour phone lines. A traveler who calls them at midnight gets an answer. A traveler who calls your company at midnight gets voicemail. The customer does not wait. They call the next number. That is the entire game, and the big brands win it while you sleep."},{"type":"p","text":"The leak is bigger than most owners think. It is not just midnight. It is the Sunday morning caller while your one staff member is washing cars. It is the Friday evening rush when three people call at once and your single phone line can only handle one. Every missed call is someone who needed a car, and most of them will not call back later. They will book elsewhere, because their trip cannot wait for your callback."},{"type":"p","text":"The car rental market is enormous and still growing. The global market was worth around one hundred fifty billion dollars in twenty twenty four, and it is expected to reach close to two hundred seventy eight billion by twenty thirty. That growth means more travelers, more bookings, and more after-hours calls. An AI chatbot is the simplest way for a small operator to answer those calls. It does not replace your team. It covers the hours your team cannot, and it handles the busy-hour overflow your team cannot get to."},{"type":"h2","text":"What customers actually ask (the same 10 questions)"},{"type":"p","text":"Strip a car rental company''s inbox down to its bones, and you will find the same ten questions, asked over and over, every single day. What is the daily rate for an economy car? Do you have an SUV available this weekend? What documents do I need to rent? How much is the security deposit, and when do I get it back? Is insurance included, or do I pay extra? Is there a mileage limit? What is your fuel policy? Can I pick up at the airport? Do you allow a second driver? What happens if I return the car late?"},{"type":"p","text":"Your staff answers these questions hundreds of times a month. Each answer takes two or three minutes. That is hours every week spent repeating the same facts. Worse, when nobody is available to answer, the question sits unanswered, and the customer moves on. An AI chatbot trained on your fleet, your rates, and your policies answers all ten questions instantly, every time, in your own words. It knows which cars are available this weekend, because it is connected to your booking calendar. It answers at midnight exactly the way your best staff member answers at noon. And when a question goes beyond the facts, a long-term lease, a damage dispute, a special request, it hands the conversation to you with the full chat attached."},{"type":"h2","text":"What the chatbot actually does, day and night"},{"type":"p","text":"Think of the chatbot as your most reliable front-desk employee, one who never sleeps and can talk to fifty customers at once. Here is what it handles around the clock."},{"type":"ul","items":["Answers every call, message, and chat instantly, on your website, on WhatsApp, and by text. No ringing, no voicemail, no waiting. The customer gets a friendly reply in seconds, even at two in the morning.","Takes bookings start to finish. It collects the pickup date and time, the return date, the car type, the pickup location, and the driver''s details, then confirms the booking with a reference number.","Quotes rates on the spot. The customer asks how much for a sedan for three days, and the chatbot gives the exact price from your real rate card, including taxes and extras, without making the customer wait for a callback.","Explains deposit, insurance, mileage, and fuel policy in plain words. It answers the questions customers are embarrassed to ask twice, patiently, every time.","Sends booking confirmations and pickup reminders. A message the day before pickup with the address, the time, and what to bring, which cuts down on confusion and late arrivals.","Follows up on quotes that went quiet. If a customer asked for a price and disappeared, the chatbot checks in a day later with a gentle nudge, and that alone recovers bookings that were silently dying.","Handles changes and cancellations gracefully. Can I move my pickup to Friday? Can I extend by one day? The chatbot checks availability and confirms the change in seconds.","Collects driver details ahead of time. License photo, contact number, second driver name, so pickup day is fast and your counter line stays short.","Recovers lapsed customers. A friendly message to people who rented six months ago and never came back brings a surprising number of them back for their next trip."]},{"type":"h2","text":"Instant quotes without the phone tag"},{"type":"p","text":"The quote is where most small rental companies lose bookings without even knowing it. A customer calls and asks for a rate. Your staff is busy, so they promise to call back. An hour later they call back, but the customer is in a meeting. They play phone tag for a day. By the time the quote lands, the customer has already booked with the company that answered first."},{"type":"p","text":"An AI chatbot ends the phone tag completely. The customer asks for a rate, and the answer comes in seconds, with the full breakdown. And because the chatbot knows it sent a quote, it follows up. One day later, a short message: still need the car for the weekend? I have the sedan held for you until tonight. That single message recovers bookings every single week. Businesses that answer inquiries instantly and follow up on quotes report processing forty to sixty percent more bookings without hiring anyone new. The customers were already there. They just needed someone to answer."},{"type":"p","text":"The chatbot also quotes honestly. It shows the real price, with taxes and the deposit clearly stated. Customers trust that, and trust is what turns a one-time renter into someone who calls you every trip. Travel agencies live and die on the same principle. See how [AI chatbots help travel agencies](/blog/ai-chatbot-travel-agencies) turn quick quotes into confirmed bookings."},{"type":"h2","text":"Follow-up: the booking you almost lost"},{"type":"p","text":"Most rental owners think a lost booking is gone forever. It is not. A large share of people who asked for a quote and went quiet still need a car. They got distracted, they were comparing prices, or they were waiting to confirm their flight. A timely follow-up brings them back."},{"type":"p","text":"The chatbot runs this follow-up automatically, and it does it well, because it remembers the conversation. It knows the customer wanted an SUV for Friday, not a generic car. So the follow-up is specific: the SUV for Friday is still available, and the weekend rate is the same as quoted. A customer who gets a message that remembers their exact request feels looked after, not spammed. This is the same patient logic behind good [lead capture automation](/blog/lead-capture-automation). The goal is not to pressure people. It is to be there, with the right answer, at the moment they are ready to decide."},{"type":"p","text":"There is a second kind of follow-up that matters just as much: the after-rental check-in. A day after the customer returns the car, the chatbot asks how the trip went. Happy customers get a gentle request for a review. Unhappy customers get heard before they write a bad review online. That single message protects your reputation and brings repeat business, and it costs your team zero minutes."},{"type":"h2","text":"Pickup day chaos — late arrivals and no-shows"},{"type":"p","text":"Pickup day is the most stressful part of running a rental company, and it is almost entirely a communication problem. Customers arrive late without warning. Some do not show up at all, and the car sits reserved and unbilled while another customer who needed it walks away."},{"type":"p","text":"The chatbot smooths all of this out. The day before pickup, it sends a reminder with the address, the pickup time, and a one-tap button to confirm or report a delay. Most customers confirm in seconds. The ones running late tap the delay button and tell you their new arrival time. The ones who need to cancel do it through the chatbot, and the car goes straight back into your available fleet. Fifteen minutes after the pickup time, it tries once more with the no-shows: are you still coming? Over a month, the recovered pickups and freed-up cars add up to real money."},{"type":"p","text":"This is also where the fleet connection matters. A lot of small operators run their fleet on one system and their customer messages on another, usually WhatsApp, with nothing connecting the two. The chatbot bridges that gap. It knows which cars are actually available, so it never promises a car you do not have. Car dealerships face the same disconnect between their stock and their messages. See how [WhatsApp automation for car dealerships](/blog/whatsapp-automation-car-dealerships) keeps the two in sync."},{"type":"h2","text":"Setting it up for a small rental business"},{"type":"p","text":"You do not need an IT department. A practical setup for a small rental company takes days, not months. First, write down your rate card and policies. Your daily and weekly rates for each car type, the security deposit, the insurance options, the mileage limit, the fuel policy, and the documents you require. Write them the way your best staff member explains them, in plain words. This becomes the chatbot''s brain."},{"type":"p","text":"Second, connect your availability. The chatbot needs to know which cars are free and which are booked, usually a simple connection to your existing booking calendar. It does not need to be fancy. It just needs to be accurate, because a chatbot that promises a car you do not have is worse than no chatbot at all."},{"type":"p","text":"Third, define your booking rules. How far ahead can someone book? Which situations need a human, like a foreign license, a monthly rental, or a damage dispute? The chatbot follows your rules exactly and hands everything else to your team with the full conversation attached."},{"type":"p","text":"Fourth, connect your channels. Your website chat, your WhatsApp Business number, and your text messages all flow to the chatbot first. One brain, every door covered. Then start with after-hours. Let it handle nights and weekends for two weeks while your team watches during the day. Add the missing answers, then turn on quote follow-ups, then pickup reminders. Each step makes the next one smoother. And show your staff the dashboard where they see every conversation. When they realize it handles the repetitive questions and leaves them the real customer service, they become its biggest supporters."},{"type":"h2","text":"Mistakes to avoid"},{"type":"p","text":"The biggest mistake is letting the chatbot quote wrong prices. Your rate card changes with seasons and demand. If the chatbot quotes last month''s rates, you will lose money or argue with customers at the counter. Keep the rate card updated, and check it monthly at minimum."},{"type":"p","text":"Second: no human escape hatch. If a customer is upset about a charge or dealing with a breakdown on the road, the chatbot must hand over to a real person immediately, with the whole conversation attached. A panicked customer arguing with a bot is a one-star review waiting to happen."},{"type":"p","text":"Third: promising cars you do not have. Always connect the chatbot to your live fleet status. One broken promise at the counter undoes a hundred good conversations. Fourth: sounding like a call center. Train it on your real replies, the way you actually text customers. Fifth: setting it up and forgetting it. Check the conversations weekly for the first month, add the missing answers, and the system gets sharper every week."},{"type":"h2","text":"What it costs vs what it saves"},{"type":"p","text":"Let us do honest math for a small rental company. Say you run twelve cars and average twenty bookings a week. Say you miss just three inquiries a week to unanswered calls and slow quotes. At an average booking value of one hundred twenty dollars, that is three hundred sixty dollars a week walking out the door, or over eighteen thousand dollars a year."},{"type":"p","text":"Now add the quote follow-ups. Say you send ten quotes a week and close four. With automatic follow-up, you close six instead. That is two extra bookings a week, another two hundred forty dollars weekly, over twelve thousand a year. Together, the recovered bookings are worth more than thirty thousand dollars a year for a twelve-car operation."},{"type":"p","text":"An AI chatbot for a small rental company costs a fraction of one part-time hire, and it works every hour of every day. It never sleeps through the midnight call, never forgets to follow up on a quote, and never leaves a customer staring at voicemail in an arrivals hall. There is also the cost nobody puts in a spreadsheet: your own evenings. Right now, the after-hours phone probably lives in your pocket. The chatbot gives you your nights back while answering customers better than a tired owner ever could at midnight."},{"type":"p","text":"Your cars are good, your prices are fair, and your service is personal. The chatbot just makes sure no traveler ever stands in an arrivals hall, listening to your voicemail, while a competitor drives them away. Curious what this looks like for your own rental company? A free custom demo shows the chatbot answering in your voice, with your rates and your fleet, built around your business, not a template. Request yours at https://replypilot-site.vercel.app/ and see it handle your first midnight inquiry."},{"type":"h3","text":"Want this working for your rental fleet?"},{"type":"p","text":"ReplyPilot builds custom AI chatbots for car rental services, trained on your fleet, your rates, and your booking rules, with a free custom demo before you decide. No strings either way."},{"type":"h2","text":"Return Day: Deposits, Damage, and the Review Request"},{"type":"p","text":"Pickup day gets all the attention, but return day is where disputes are born. The customer is tired, the flight is in three hours, and nobody explained the deposit. The chatbot fixes this before it starts. The evening before return, it sends a reminder: return time, the fuel level expected, and where to leave the keys. At return, it explains the deposit clearly — how much, when it comes back, and what could delay it. If the inspection finds a scratch, the chatbot logs photos and notes from both sides — but it never decides who pays. That decision is yours, with the full record attached. A clear process beats a tired argument at the counter."},{"type":"p","text":"The day after return comes the message that protects your reputation: a simple check-in on the trip, and a review request for happy customers. Travellers write reviews while the trip is fresh — catch them then. And if a customer is unhappy, you hear about it in your inbox before it appears on a public review site, which is exactly where you want it."},{"type":"h2","text":"The Monthly Renter: Your Steadiest Income"},{"type":"p","text":"Not every customer rents for a weekend. Contractors on long jobs, companies with visiting staff, locals between cars — monthly renters are the steadiest income a small rental company has, and they need a different conversation. The chatbot handles the longer flow: monthly rates, payment schedules, who handles servicing during a long rental, and mileage terms. For companies, it collects the business details and hands the account setup to you. These customers book rarely and pay reliably — treat them like the regulars they are, because they are."},{"type":"h2","text":"Frequently Asked Questions"},{"type":"h3","text":"Can an AI chatbot quote car rental prices instantly?"},{"type":"p","text":"Yes. It asks for dates, car type, and pickup location, then quotes from your real fleet and pricing rules. Travellers compare several companies at once — the instant quote usually wins."},{"type":"h3","text":"How much does a chatbot cost for a small car rental company?"},{"type":"p","text":"Usually a monthly fee for car rental, based on conversation volume. A single rescued booking — the kind you used to lose to a missed call — often covers the cost. Small fleets see fast payback because every booking matters."},{"type":"h3","text":"Can the car rental chatbot take bookings at night?"},{"type":"p","text":"That is when it earns its keep. Flights land late, plans change, and travellers book at midnight. The chatbot confirms availability and takes the booking while you sleep. You wake up to confirmed reservations."},{"type":"h3","text":"Will the chatbot know my real fleet availability?"},{"type":"p","text":"Yes — it connects to your booking system or calendar, not a made-up list. It only offers cars that are actually free. This avoids the embarrassment of confirming a car you do not have."},{"type":"h3","text":"Can the rental chatbot handle add-ons like child seats and extra drivers?"},{"type":"p","text":"Yes. During booking it asks about child seats, additional drivers, and insurance options, adding them to the quote. These add-ons raise each booking''s value without any extra work from you."}]'::jsonb, ARRAY['AI chatbot for car rental services', 'car rental booking automation', 'car rental missed calls', 'rental car quote follow-up', 'car rental WhatsApp chatbot']::text[], NULL, TRUE, '2026-10-10T00:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-10T00:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, 3110, 19645)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, keywords, cover_image, published, created_at, updated_at, word_count, content_chars)
VALUES ('ai-chatbot-barbershops', 'AI Chatbot for Barbershops: Fill Every Chair Hands-Free', 'Book appointments, answer price questions, fill last-minute cancellations and bring regulars back - while you''re mid-fade.', '[{"type":"img","src":"/blog/ai-chatbot-barbershops.jpg","alt":"Barber doing a skin fade while his phone buzzes with unread booking messages he cannot answer mid-cut","caption":"A barber mid-fade can''t answer the buzzing phone full of booking requests."},{"type":"p","text":"It''s Saturday at 11am. You''re halfway through a skin fade, clippers humming, and your phone lights up on the counter. Then it buzzes again. And again. Each buzz is money trying to reach you - a beard trim for Friday evening, a father-and-son cut for Sunday morning, a wedding party of six asking about group bookings. But your hands are covered in hair and product, and every one of those messages waits. By the time you wipe down, sanitize, and finally check your phone at 1pm, two of those people have already booked with the shop down the street. The third one? He found someone on Instagram who replied in four minutes. This is the daily reality of running a barbershop in 2026: your craft is in your hands, but your bookings live in a phone you can''t touch for eight hours a day. An AI chatbot for barbershops changes this completely. It answers every message in seconds, quotes your real prices, books into your actual calendar, and fills cancellations before the chair goes cold - while you keep cutting."},{"type":"h2","text":"Why Barbershops Lose Bookings Every Single Day"},{"type":"p","text":"Let''s be honest about how bookings actually work in most barbershops. A guy sees your work on Instagram - a clean taper, a sharp beard lineup - and he messages you. He''s ready. He''s already decided. He just needs three things: the price, a time that works, and confirmation. If he gets all three within ten minutes, he''s yours. If he waits three hours, he''s still probably yours. If he waits until tomorrow? He''s gone. He''s found another barber who answered faster, and he''ll probably never message you again. This isn''t a theory. Ask any barber who''s checked their DMs after a busy Saturday - the unread messages from Friday night are bookings that died quietly while they were working."},{"type":"p","text":"The math is brutal. Say you miss just two bookings a week because of slow replies. At an average of $35 per cut, that''s $70 a week, $280 a month, $3,360 a year - walking out the door because your hands were busy doing the actual work. And that''s conservative. Busy shops in good locations lose far more. The painful irony is that the better you are at cutting hair, the more messages pile up, and the more bookings you lose. Success literally creates the problem. Your Instagram grows, your reputation spreads, more guys message you - and more of them slip through the cracks because there''s only one of you and you can only hold clippers or a phone, not both."},{"type":"p","text":"Then there''s the timing problem. Men book haircuts at odd hours. The data from booking platforms consistently shows that a huge share of barbershop bookings happen outside business hours - late evenings, early mornings, Sunday nights when guys are planning their week. Your shop is closed. You''re asleep, or with family, or finally resting your feet. But the guy messaging at 11pm on Sunday is planning his Monday, and if your competitor''s chatbot books him in 30 seconds while your phone sits silent until morning, you''ve lost him. Not because you''re a worse barber. Because you were asleep, which is completely reasonable, and completely costly."},{"type":"h2","text":"What an AI Chatbot for Barbershops Actually Does"},{"type":"p","text":"Forget the sci-fi version of a barbershop bot. An AI chatbot for barbershops is a messaging assistant that lives in your WhatsApp or Instagram DMs - the places guys already message you - and handles the booking conversation the way your best receptionist would, if you had one working 24 hours a day. Beauty businesses run on the same Instagram booking rush - see how [Instagram DM automation for salons](/blog/instagram-dm-automation-salons) turns DMs into appointments. When someone messages ''how much for a fade?'', it doesn''t send a generic reply. It knows your actual price list: skin fade $35, beard trim $20, the combo $50, kids cuts $25. It answers in your voice, the way you actually text, not like a corporate robot."},{"type":"p","text":"When the guy says ''okay, Saturday morning?'', the chatbot checks your real calendar - the same one you use, whether that''s a booking app, Google Calendar, or a simple schedule you set - and offers actual open slots. ''I have 10:30 or 11:15 open Saturday - which works?'' He picks one. The chatbot confirms it, sends him a reminder the day before, and logs it where you can see it. The whole conversation takes 45 seconds. You never touched your phone once. You were doing a hot towel shave the entire time, and a booking landed in your calendar like it was always going to."},{"type":"p","text":"It also handles the questions that eat your evenings. ''Do you do walk-ins?'' ''How long does a cut take?'' ''Where are you located?'' ''Do you cut kids hair?'' These are the same five questions, asked fifty times a week, and every one of them used to cost you two minutes of thumb-typing between clients or after hours. The chatbot answers all of them instantly, from your real information. And here''s what matters: it knows when to hand over. If someone asks something complicated - a wedding party booking, a complaint, something unusual - it doesn''t guess. It says ''Let me have the boss take a look at this one'' and flags it for you with the full conversation attached. You handle the exceptions. It handles everything else."},{"type":"h2","text":"Filling Cancellations Before the Chair Goes Cold"},{"type":"p","text":"Every barber knows the sting of a no-show. You blocked 45 minutes. You turned away a walk-in because the slot was ''taken.'' Then the guy never shows, never messages, and that chair sat empty earning nothing. Industry data suggests barbershops lose 10 to 15 percent of booked slots to no-shows and late cancellations. For a shop doing 30 cuts a week, that''s 3 to 4 empty chairs weekly - potentially $100 to $140 gone, every single week, for no reason except that nobody followed up."},{"type":"p","text":"A chatbot attacks this from two directions. First, prevention: it sends confirmation messages when someone books (''You''re locked in for Saturday 10:30 - reply YES to confirm''), and a reminder the day before (''See you tomorrow at 10:30! Reply if you need to reschedule''). Guys who get reminders show up far more often. It''s not complicated psychology - it''s just that people forget, and a nudge fixes forgetting. Shops using automated reminders typically cut no-shows dramatically, and every saved appointment is pure recovered revenue."},{"type":"p","text":"Second, recovery: when someone does cancel, the chatbot doesn''t just accept the empty slot. It immediately messages your waitlist - guys who asked about that day, regulars who usually come around then. ''A 2pm slot just opened today - want it?'' The first one to reply gets it. In busy shops, cancelled slots get refilled within minutes instead of sitting empty. Think about what that means over a year. If you recover even two cancelled slots a week at $35 each, that''s $3,640 a year in revenue that used to evaporate. The chatbot pays for itself many times over just on this one job."},{"type":"ul","items":["Booking confirmation the moment someone reserves - locks in commitment","Reminder 24 hours before - cuts forgetful no-shows","Same-day reminder option for chronic latecomers","Instant waitlist outreach when a slot opens - refills chairs in minutes","Easy reschedule flow - keeps the revenue instead of losing the client"]},{"type":"h2","text":"Bringing Regulars Back Without Being Pushy"},{"type":"p","text":"Here''s something most barbers don''t think about: your best marketing asset isn''t Instagram. It''s your existing client list. A regular who gets a cut every three weeks is worth roughly $600 a year. If he drifts to every five weeks because life gets busy and nobody reminded him, he''s suddenly worth $360. Multiply that drift across twenty regulars and you''re quietly losing thousands a year - not to competitors, just to forgetfulness. Nail salons face the same rebooking drift - [Instagram DM automation for nail salons](/blog/instagram-dm-automation-nail-salons) shows how appointment-based beauty businesses keep clients on schedule."},{"type":"p","text":"A chatbot fixes this gently. It knows when each client last came in - because it has the booking history - and it nudges at the right moment. Five weeks since his last fade? ''Hey Marcus, it''s been a minute - looking sharp still or time for a cleanup? I have Thursday evening open.'' That''s not spam. That''s the digital version of what great barbers have always done - remembering their guys and looking out for them. The difference is that now it happens automatically, for every client, without you having to track any of it in your head."},{"type":"p","text":"It can also run simple win-back campaigns. Guys who haven''t booked in two months get a friendly check-in, maybe with a small incentive: ''Miss you in the chair - $5 off your next cut this week.'' Birthday messages go out automatically. Slow Tuesday afternoons get filled by offering waitlist clients first dibs on discounted slots. None of this requires you to become a marketer. You set the rules once - who gets nudged, when, with what offer - and the chatbot runs it quietly in the background while you cut hair."},{"type":"h2","text":"Keeping Your Shop''s Personality (This Part Matters Most)"},{"type":"p","text":"Barbershops run on personality. Guys don''t just come for the cut - they come for the vibe, the conversation, the feeling that this is their spot. A chatbot that sounds like a bank''s customer service line would poison that. ''Thank you for your inquiry. How may I assist you today?'' - no. Absolutely not. The chatbot needs to sound like your shop. If your Instagram captions are casual and funny, the chatbot is casual and funny. If you''re a premium shop with a refined tone, it matches that."},{"type":"p","text":"This is done by training it on your real voice. The setup process involves feeding it examples of how you actually talk to clients - your real messages, your real replies, your real personality. It learns your prices, your services, your hours, your policies, your jokes. When a regular messages, it can even recognize him: ''What''s good, D - the usual fade Thursday?'' That level of personal touch is what separates a great barbershop chatbot from a generic booking bot, and it''s the difference between clients loving it and tolerating it."},{"type":"p","text":"There''s also a trust element. Guys need to know they''re talking to your shop, not some random company. The chatbot introduces itself naturally, uses your shop''s name, references your real services. And it''s always clear that a human - you - is behind it and available. ''If you need anything else, Marcus is right here in the shop.'' The technology disappears into the experience. Clients just notice that your shop replies fast, remembers them, and never drops the ball. That''s not a tech feature. That''s just good service, delivered consistently."},{"type":"h2","text":"What It Costs vs. What It Saves (Honest Numbers)"},{"type":"p","text":"Let''s talk money plainly, because that''s what matters. A part-time receptionist for a barbershop costs $1,500 to $2,500 a month, and they still go home at night - they don''t answer the 11pm Sunday messages. A booking app subscription runs $30 to $100 a month, but apps don''t answer questions, don''t fill cancellations, and don''t bring regulars back. They just sit there waiting for the client to do all the work. An AI chatbot service typically costs a fraction of a receptionist''s salary - and it works every hour of every day."},{"type":"p","text":"Now the other side. We calculated earlier: two saved bookings a week is $3,360 a year. Two recovered cancellations a week is another $3,640. Bringing just five drifting regulars back to their normal schedule could be $1,200 a year. Add it up and a busy shop could be looking at $8,000 to $10,000 in recovered annual revenue - from messages that were already coming in, from clients who already wanted to book. The chatbot doesn''t create demand. It stops demand from leaking. That''s a much easier and more reliable return."},{"type":"p","text":"The setup takes days, not months. You provide your price list, your hours, your booking calendar access, and examples of how you talk. The chatbot is trained, tested with real conversations, and then it goes live in your WhatsApp or Instagram. You watch the first week''s conversations to make sure the tone is right, tweak anything that feels off, and then it runs. Most barbers report that within the first month, they notice the difference in one specific way: they stop feeling anxious about their phone. The unread message count stops climbing during busy Saturdays. That peace of mind, honestly, is worth as much as the revenue."},{"type":"h2","text":"Getting Started: What You Need (It''s Less Than You Think)"},{"type":"p","text":"You don''t need new software, new hardware, or any technical skill. Here''s the actual list: your price list (you know it by heart), your working hours, whatever calendar you use for bookings (even a paper book can work - it just gets digitized), and about an hour of your time to review how the chatbot talks. That''s it. The whole thing runs inside WhatsApp or Instagram - the apps your clients already use. Nobody downloads anything. Nobody learns anything new."},{"type":"ul","items":["Your service menu with real prices - fade, taper, beard, combo, kids","Your working hours and days off - including holiday schedules","Your booking calendar - app, Google Calendar, or even a photo of your book","Examples of your real messages - so it learns your voice","Your policies - deposits, late arrivals, no-show rules"]},{"type":"p","text":"One thing to decide upfront: deposits. Many shops now take small deposits for bookings ($10-$15) to cut no-shows, and a chatbot can handle this smoothly - sending the payment link right in the chat, confirming only when paid. If you don''t want deposits, that''s fine too. The chatbot works either way. The point is that these are your business decisions; the technology just executes them consistently, every time, without you having to remember or enforce anything in the moment."},{"type":"h2","text":"Real Scenarios: A Week With the Chatbot"},{"type":"p","text":"Monday 9pm: You''re home, feet up, done for the day. A message comes in: ''Yo, you got anything Thursday after 6?'' The chatbot checks your calendar, sees the 6:30 slot open, and books it. You find out Tuesday morning when you glance at your schedule. You did nothing. The booking happened while you were watching TV."},{"type":"p","text":"Wednesday 2pm: You''re mid-cut with a regular when a new guy messages asking about prices for a full service - cut, beard, eyebrows. The chatbot sends your full menu, answers his follow-up about how long it takes, and books him for Friday at 4pm. The regular in your chair never noticed your phone buzz. The new client never waited."},{"type":"p","text":"Friday 10am: A guy booked for 11am messages: ''Running 20 late, still okay?'' The chatbot knows your late policy (15 minutes, then the slot goes to waitlist). It tells him honestly, offers to move him to 2pm, and simultaneously messages two waitlist guys about the 11am opening. One of them takes it. Your 11am chair stays full. The late guy still gets his cut at 2pm. Nobody lost. Everybody won."},{"type":"p","text":"Saturday 6pm: Your last client leaves. You check your phone - not with dread, but curiosity. Four new bookings for next week. Two reschedules handled. One waitlist slot filled. Zero unread messages that need your attention. You put the phone down and go home. This is what the technology is actually for - not replacing you, but removing the part of the job you never signed up for: being a full-time receptionist on top of being a full-time barber."},{"type":"h2","text":"Common Worries (Answered Honestly)"},{"type":"p","text":"''My clients want to talk to ME, not a robot.'' They''re right - and they still will. The chatbot handles bookings and questions. The actual relationship - the conversation in the chair, the trust, the craft - that''s all you, untouched. Think of it this way: your clients already prefer texting over calling. The chatbot just makes sure someone always texts back. When they sit in your chair, it''s 100% human, 100% you."},{"type":"p","text":"''What if it says something wrong?'' It works from your real information - your prices, your hours, your policies. It doesn''t invent things. And for anything it''s unsure about, it''s programmed to hand over to you rather than guess. You also review conversations, especially in the first few weeks, and correct anything that feels off. It learns. Within a month, it handles 90% of messages without you ever looking."},{"type":"p","text":"''I''m not good with technology.'' You don''t need to be. There''s no dashboard to learn, no software to install, no new habits to build. It lives in WhatsApp or Instagram - apps you already use every day. The setup is a conversation: someone asks you about your prices, hours, and how you talk to clients, and they build it from that. If you can send a text message, you can run this."},{"type":"h3","text":"Want the same running in your barbershop?"},{"type":"p","text":"The barbers winning with this aren''t tech people. They''re craftsmen who got tired of losing bookings to a phone they couldn''t answer. The moment it clicks is usually the first Saturday after going live - you''re deep in back-to-back cuts, your phone is face-down on the counter, and at the end of the day you check it to find three new bookings that happened while you were working. Nobody waited. Nobody left. The chairs filled themselves. If you want to see exactly what this looks like with your own prices, your own hours, and your own shop''s personality, ReplyPilot will build you a free custom demo: request it at https://replypilot-site.vercel.app/ and watch your next busy Saturday handle itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Personal-care bookings start with a DM — [Instagram DM automation for tattoo studios](/blog/instagram-dm-automation-tattoo-studios) answers flash, pricing and aftercare questions automatically."},{"type":"h2","text":"Frequently Asked Questions"},{"type":"h3","text":"Can an AI chatbot fill last-minute barbershop cancellations?"},{"type":"p","text":"Yes. When someone cancels, it instantly messages your waitlist with the freed slot. Empty chairs earn nothing, and this fills them within minutes. You set the waitlist rules once."},{"type":"h3","text":"How much does a chatbot cost for a barbershop?"},{"type":"p","text":"Usually a modest monthly fee for a barbershop. A single filled cancellation covers it. Barbershops with 3+ chairs see the fastest return because empty chairs multiply."},{"type":"h3","text":"Can the barbershop chatbot take bookings after closing time?"},{"type":"p","text":"Absolutely. Guys decide at 10pm they need a cut tomorrow. The chatbot books them into your real schedule. Morning brings a full book instead of missed DMs."},{"type":"h3","text":"Will the chatbot know each barber''s schedule?"},{"type":"p","text":"Yes. You load each barber''s hours and services. Customers pick their barber or take whoever is free. No more double-bookings or \"I thought Ali was working today\" confusion."},{"type":"h3","text":"Can the chatbot send haircut reminders to regulars?"},{"type":"p","text":"Yes. It messages regulars when their usual cut is due — \"It''s been 4 weeks, want your usual Friday slot?\" Regulars are a barbershop''s backbone, and reminders keep them coming without you remembering."}]'::jsonb, ARRAY['AI chatbot for barbershops', 'barbershop appointment booking automation', 'barber shop chatbot for bookings']::text[], NULL, TRUE, '2026-10-09T00:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-09T00:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, 3135, 19856)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, keywords, cover_image, published, created_at, updated_at, word_count, content_chars)
VALUES ('whatsapp-automation-pharmacies', 'WhatsApp Automation for Pharmacies: 5 Counter Wins', 'Handle prescription refills, send dose reminders and answer stock questions - and take the phone off your counter for good.', '[{"type":"img","src":"/blog/whatsapp-automation-pharmacies.jpg","alt":"Pharmacist counting tablets while the phone rings and WhatsApp refill questions pile up unread","caption":"A pharmacist counts tablets while WhatsApp refill questions pile up unanswered."},{"type":"p","text":"It''s 6pm and your pharmacy is at its busiest. The counter has four people waiting. You''re counting tablets for a prescription, and the phone rings. You let it go - you have to, your hands are full. Then a WhatsApp message buzzes: ''Is my blood pressure medicine ready?'' Then another: ''Do you have children''s fever syrup in stock?'' Then the phone rings again. Every interruption slows down the actual dispensing, the work that genuinely needs a pharmacist''s brain. Meanwhile the people at the counter wait longer, and the people on WhatsApp wait even longer. This is the daily reality of running a community pharmacy: you''re a healthcare professional spending half your day answering the same logistical questions that a messaging assistant could handle in seconds. WhatsApp automation for pharmacies takes the repetitive work off your counter so you can focus on the work that actually needs you."},{"type":"h2","text":"The Refill Problem (And Why It''s Costing You)"},{"type":"p","text":"Most pharmacy revenue comes from repeat prescriptions. Blood pressure tablets, diabetes medication, thyroid pills - the same patients, the same medicines, every 30 or 90 days, month after month. This should be the easiest, most predictable part of your business. Instead, it''s a constant scramble. Patients forget to request refills. They call when they''ve already run out. They show up at the counter frustrated because they need it today and it''s not ready. You rush to prepare it while the queue grows. Nobody''s happy, and it was all preventable."},{"type":"p","text":"Here''s what actually happens with refills: a patient takes their last tablet on Sunday night, realizes Monday morning, and calls you in a panic. Or worse - they skip doses for two days because they kept forgetting to call, which is genuinely bad for their health. Studies on medication adherence consistently show that a huge percentage of patients miss doses simply because the refill process has friction. Every step - remembering, calling, waiting, picking up - is a chance to drop off. An automated WhatsApp flow removes almost all of that friction."},{"type":"p","text":"The system works like this: when a patient starts a repeat prescription, the automation notes the cycle. A few days before they run out, they get a WhatsApp message: ''Hi Ahmed, your blood pressure tablets run out in 3 days. Shall I prepare your refill?'' They tap yes. It''s prepared. They get a message when it''s ready: ''Your refill is ready for pickup - or reply DELIVER and we''ll send it today.'' The patient never runs out. You never get a panic call. The revenue from that prescription - which was always yours - actually arrives instead of leaking to the pharmacy down the street that happened to answer the phone faster."},{"type":"p","text":"The health angle matters too, and it''s worth saying plainly. Missed doses for blood pressure, diabetes, and heart medication cause real harm - hospital visits that didn''t need to happen. A pharmacy that helps patients stay on their medication isn''t just making money; it''s doing its actual job better. When you tell patients about the refill reminders, frame it that way. ''We''ll make sure you never run out'' is a healthcare promise, not a marketing line. Patients feel cared for. And cared-for patients don''t switch pharmacies."},{"type":"h2","text":"The Questions You Answer Fifty Times a Day"},{"type":"p","text":"Let''s list them, because every pharmacist reading this will recognize every single one. ''What are your timings?'' ''Are you open on Sunday?'' ''Do you have this medicine in stock?'' ''How much is it?'' ''Do you deliver to my area?'' ''Can I pay by card?'' These are not clinical questions. They don''t need a pharmacist. But they currently consume hours of pharmacist time every week, because there''s been no alternative - until now. Dental clinics deal with the same flood of logistical questions - [AI chatbots for dental clinics](/blog/ai-chatbots-dental-clinics) shows how another healthcare practice handles them."},{"type":"p","text":"A WhatsApp assistant trained on your real information answers all of these instantly. Your hours, your location, your delivery areas, your payment options - it''s all loaded in. When someone asks ''do you have Augmentin 625mg?'', it checks your actual stock and answers honestly: ''Yes, we have it - $8.50 for 20 tablets. Want me to reserve a pack?'' If it''s out of stock, it says so and offers to message when it''s back, or suggests the nearest alternative. No guessing. No ''let me check and call you back.'' Just answers, in seconds, at any hour."},{"type":"p","text":"The after-hours value is enormous. Pharmacies get messages at all hours - a parent at midnight wondering if you have children''s fever medicine, someone at 6am asking if you''re open yet. Before automation, these messages sat unread until morning, and by then the person had gone elsewhere or the moment had passed. Now they get answered immediately. ''Yes, we''re open 8am-10pm daily, and we have that in stock - want me to keep one aside for morning pickup?'' That parent remembers which pharmacy helped at midnight. That''s not a transaction. That''s loyalty being built while you sleep."},{"type":"ul","items":["Opening hours and holiday schedules - answered instantly, always current","Stock checks from your real inventory - honest yes/no with prices","Delivery areas and charges - no more ''let me ask'' delays","Payment methods accepted - card, cash, transfer, insurance","Prescription upload instructions - how to send a photo of the prescription"]},{"type":"h2","text":"Dose Reminders: The Feature Patients Actually Thank You For"},{"type":"p","text":"Beyond refills, there''s an even more personal automation: dose reminders. Patients on complex regimens - elderly patients taking six different tablets, for instance - often struggle to remember what to take when. A WhatsApp automation can send gentle reminders: ''Good morning - time for your blood pressure tablet and vitamin D.'' It''s simple. It''s not medical advice. It''s just a nudge, and patients consistently say it''s one of the most appreciated things a pharmacy can do."},{"type":"p","text":"This works especially well for new prescriptions. When someone starts a new medication, the first two weeks determine whether they''ll stick with it. Automated check-ins - ''How are you finding the new tablets? Any questions for the pharmacist?'' - catch problems early. If the patient reports a side effect, the message is flagged for you immediately. You''re not replacing the consultation; you''re extending it beyond the counter. The patient feels looked after. You catch issues before they become reasons to stop the medication."},{"type":"p","text":"For pharmacies, this is also a powerful differentiator. Most pharmacies compete on price or location. Very few compete on care. A pharmacy that reminds you to take your tablets, warns you before you run out, and checks in when you start something new - that''s a pharmacy patients recommend to their families. Word of mouth in healthcare is everything, and it''s built on exactly these small moments of feeling looked after."},{"type":"h2","text":"Prescription Uploads Without the Chaos"},{"type":"p","text":"Patients sending prescription photos over WhatsApp is already happening in most pharmacies - messily. Photos arrive at all hours, in random order, with no context. ''Whose is this?'' ''What do they need?'' ''Did anyone reply to this one?'' The automation brings order to this chaos. When a prescription photo arrives, the assistant acknowledges it immediately: ''Got it - I''ll have the pharmacist review this. What''s your name and when do you need it by?'' It collects the essential details, logs the request, and flags it for you in an organized queue."},{"type":"p","text":"You review the prescriptions in batches - say, every two hours - instead of being interrupted by each one. You confirm what''s in stock, note any issues, and the assistant messages the patient: ''Your prescription is ready - total $24. Pick up anytime before 10pm, or reply DELIVER.'' The patient gets a smooth experience. You get your focus back. The prescription that used to cause three interruptions now causes zero."},{"type":"p","text":"This also creates a record. Every prescription request, every confirmation, every pickup - logged automatically. No more ''but I sent it on Tuesday!'' disputes. No more lost photos. If there''s ever a question about what was requested and when, the history is right there. For a healthcare business, that kind of traceability isn''t just convenient - it''s proper practice."},{"type":"h2","text":"What the Automation Will Never Do (Safety First)"},{"type":"p","text":"Let''s be clear about boundaries, because this is healthcare. The WhatsApp assistant handles logistics: hours, stock, prices, refills, reminders, prescription intake. It does NOT give medical advice. It does not recommend medicines. It does not interpret symptoms. It does not adjust dosages. These lines are programmed in firmly, and the assistant is designed to recognize when a message crosses into clinical territory. Physiotherapy clinics draw the same line - [AI chatbots for physiotherapy clinics](/blog/ai-chatbot-physiotherapy-clinics) shows how they automate bookings while keeping clinical work human."},{"type":"p","text":"When someone messages ''my child has a fever, what should I give?'', the assistant doesn''t answer the medical question. It says: ''I can''t advise on that - but our pharmacist is available. They''re in the shop now / they''ll be in at 9am. For urgent concerns, please call...'' and it flags the message for you immediately. This isn''t a limitation - it''s the correct design. The automation handles everything around the clinical work so you have more time for the clinical work itself."},{"type":"p","text":"You also stay in control. Every automated conversation is visible to you. You can step into any chat at any time. Flagged messages - side effects, complaints, anything unusual - come to you instantly. The weekly review takes 15 minutes: scan the flagged items, check that the tone feels right, adjust anything. You''re the pharmacist. The assistant is your most reliable staff member - one that never sleeps, never forgets, and never gives medical opinions."},{"type":"h2","text":"The Business Case (Real Numbers)"},{"type":"p","text":"Let''s do the math honestly. A pharmacy losing just 5% of repeat prescriptions to competitors or forgetfulness - on a base of, say, 500 repeat patients averaging $30 a month - is losing $750 a month, $9,000 a year. That''s not dramatic; that''s conservative. Refill automation alone typically recovers most of that. Add the new patients who choose you because you answered at midnight when nobody else did. Add the prescription uploads that now convert instead of getting lost. The revenue impact is real and measurable."},{"type":"p","text":"On the cost side, consider what you''re currently spending on the problem. If you or your staff spend even one hour a day on repetitive phone and WhatsApp queries, that''s 30 hours a month of skilled time doing unskilled work. The automation costs a fraction of one staff member''s salary and handles the volume of three. It''s not about replacing people - it''s about letting your people do the work only they can do, while the routine work handles itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Setup is straightforward. You provide your medicine list with prices, your hours, your delivery areas, and your refill protocols. The assistant is trained on your real inventory and your real voice. Testing takes a week - you watch the conversations, correct anything, and then it runs. Most pharmacies notice the difference within days, in one specific way: the phone stops ringing for things that aren''t urgent. The counter moves faster. And the WhatsApp that used to be a source of stress becomes a source of orders."},{"type":"h2","text":"Chronic Patients: Your Most Valuable (and Most Neglected) Group"},{"type":"p","text":"Think about your top 50 patients - the ones with diabetes, hypertension, thyroid conditions who come every single month. Together they might represent 30-40% of your revenue. They''re also the most likely to drift. Not because they''re unhappy, but because managing a chronic condition is exhausting and the pharmacy is one more thing to remember. Every month, a few of them delay their refill by a week. Some switch to a pharmacy that''s closer to their new office. Some just... stop coming, and you never find out why."},{"type":"p","text":"Automation changes this relationship fundamentally. Instead of the patient having to remember you, you remember them. The WhatsApp flow for chronic patients is warmer and more personal: it uses their name, references their specific medication, and checks in like a caring human would. ''Hi Fatima, hope you''re doing well - your thyroid tablets run out Thursday. Want me to have them ready Wednesday?'' This isn''t a corporate notification. It''s the digital equivalent of what the best neighborhood pharmacists have always done - knowing their regulars. Now it scales to every regular, automatically."},{"type":"p","text":"You can also layer in simple care programs. New diabetes diagnosis? A 4-week WhatsApp sequence: week 1, how to take the medication; week 2, diet tips your pharmacist approves; week 3, reminder about the follow-up blood test; week 4, refill reminder. Every message is pre-approved by you, educational (not advisory), and ends with ''questions? Our pharmacist is here.'' Patients in these programs show measurably better adherence - and they tell their doctors, who tell other patients, who come to your pharmacy. It''s a growth loop built on genuine care."},{"type":"h2","text":"Seasonal Surges Without the Stress"},{"type":"p","text":"Every pharmacy knows the seasonal waves. Flu season brings a flood of ''do you have Tamiflu?'' and ''what''s good for this cough?'' Allergy season means antihistamine questions on repeat. Winter means vitamin D. Summer means sunscreen and rehydration salts. During these surges, your phone becomes unusable - it''s just constant interruptions while you''re trying to serve the people physically in front of you."},{"type":"p","text":"The automation absorbs these waves. Before flu season, you load in the relevant information: which antivirals you stock, current prices, what requires a prescription versus what''s over the counter. When the surge hits, the assistant handles the repetitive questions while you handle the counter. ''Yes, we have it in stock - $12. Want me to reserve one? We''re open till 10pm.'' The patient gets served. You stay focused. And because the assistant tracks what''s being asked, you get a useful signal: if 40 people asked about a specific medicine this week, you know to order more before you run out."},{"type":"p","text":"This demand sensing is an underrated benefit. The automation quietly logs every stock question, creating a real-time picture of what your community needs. That data helps you order smarter, avoid stockouts on high-demand items, and spot trends before they become emergencies. It''s market research generated automatically from conversations you were already having - you just weren''t capturing them before."},{"type":"h2","text":"Getting Started for Your Pharmacy This Week"},{"type":"p","text":"You don''t need new systems or technical skills. The automation lives in WhatsApp - the app your patients already use. Here''s what you provide: your stock list (even a spreadsheet works), your hours, your delivery zones and charges, and your refill rules (how many days before running out should we remind?). That''s the core. Everything else - the tone, the message templates, the escalation rules - is built around how your pharmacy already works."},{"type":"ul","items":["Your medicine list with current prices - spreadsheet is fine","Opening hours including weekends and holidays","Delivery areas, charges, and minimum order if any","Refill reminder timing - 3 days before? 5 days?","Your prescription review process - how often you check new uploads"]},{"type":"p","text":"Start with refills - it''s the highest-impact, lowest-risk automation. Once that''s running smoothly and patients are responding well, add stock questions, then prescription intake, then dose reminders. Each layer builds on the last. Within a month, the repetitive work that used to eat your days is handled, and you''re spending your time on the things that made you become a pharmacist in the first place."},{"type":"h3","text":"Want this working in your pharmacy?"},{"type":"p","text":"The pharmacies thriving with this aren''t tech companies. They''re neighborhood pharmacies that got tired of watching patients slip away over missed messages and forgotten refills. The moment it clicks is usually the first Monday after going live - you open your phone to find a queue of confirmed refills, answered stock questions, and prescription uploads all neatly organized, instead of the usual chaos. Your patients got served while you were closed. If you want to see exactly what this looks like with your own medicines, your own prices, and your own refill protocols, ReplyPilot will build you a free custom demo: request it at https://replypilot-site.vercel.app/ and watch your pharmacy run itself this week."},{"type":"h2","text":"Frequently Asked Questions"},{"type":"h3","text":"Can WhatsApp automation handle prescription refills for my pharmacy?"},{"type":"p","text":"Yes. Customers message \"Refill my blood pressure tablets,\" and the automation confirms the prescription, checks stock, and tells them when to collect. Refill customers are your most loyal — make it effortless for them."},{"type":"h3","text":"How much does WhatsApp automation cost for a pharmacy?"},{"type":"p","text":"Typically a monthly fee based on message volume. Pharmacies handle huge message volumes, so the per-message value is high. A single retained refill customer covers the cost."},{"type":"h3","text":"Can it send medicine dose reminders to pharmacy customers?"},{"type":"p","text":"Yes. Customers opt in, and the automation reminds them to take their doses. This improves health outcomes and builds fierce loyalty. Elderly customers especially appreciate it."},{"type":"h3","text":"Will the automation answer \"Do you have this medicine in stock?\""},{"type":"p","text":"Yes — it checks your inventory and answers instantly. Customers stop calling for stock checks, and your counter staff focuses on the people standing in front of them."},{"type":"h3","text":"Can the pharmacy chatbot take orders for home delivery?"},{"type":"p","text":"Yes. It collects the order, confirms the address, and queues it for your delivery run. Home delivery customers order more often because it is easy. The automation makes easy even easier."}]'::jsonb, ARRAY['WhatsApp automation for pharmacies', 'pharmacy prescription refill automation', 'pharmacy chatbot refill reminders']::text[], NULL, TRUE, '2026-10-09T00:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '2026-10-09T00:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, 2864, 19085)
